  "ground_truth": {
    "root_cause": "billing-api was migrated to a new host pool overnight and its DNS A records were updated from 10.4.1.x to 10.9.2.x, with the old hosts shut down at 08:00. Some client resolvers had cached the old records with a 3600s TTL and the change was less than an hour old, so ~25% of callers were still resolving billing-api to the old, now-dead addresses. For those clients, DNS resolution succeeded but connections went to decommissioned hosts and were refused or timed out. billing-api on the new pool is healthy. The cause is stale DNS caches outliving the old hosts after the migration. Recovery required flushing the affected resolver caches (or temporarily keeping the old IPs pointed at the new pool).",
    "evidence_path": [
      "platform-net INFO ~1h before: billing-api migrated, DNS updated 10.4.1.x -> 10.9.2.x, old hosts shut down at 08:00",
      "orders-api ERROR: connections to billing-api at 10.4.1.14 refused - that address no longer responds",
      "platform-net WARN: some clients still resolving billing-api to the old pool; resolver TTL 3600s, records changed <1h ago",
      "billing-api on the new pool is healthy; clients that re-resolved are fine; ~25% still on stale cache",
      "errors clear after resolver caches are flushed / old IPs temporarily re-pointed at the new pool"
    ],
    "fix": "Flush affected resolver caches and keep the old IPs pointed at the new pool during the transition (done). Lower DNS TTLs well before a planned migration and keep old addresses live until at least one full TTL has passed after the cutover.",
    "should_escalate": false
  }
